          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Sheep primarily symbolize God’s people—Israel, believers, or the church—who belong to God or Christ as Shepherd and hear, follow, and depend upon him.\",\"book_summary\":\"Sheep are the covenant people who hear the Shepherd’s voice and follow him.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"God’s people or followers of Christ as the Shepherd’s flock.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The covenant people as those who hear and follow the Shepherd.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book selects the Johannine marks of hearing and following but does not clearly exclude cases covered by the consensus’s central identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify sheep with God’s covenant people in relation to God or Christ as Shepherd.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s wording is a concise version of the settled primary interpretation, especially as expressed in John 10. “Covenant people” corresponds to the consensus’s “God’s people,” while hearing the Shepherd’s voice and following him are standard features already included in the consensus. The book does not replace the referent or materially alter its extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Calling sheep specifically those who hear and follow might be read as narrowing the symbol to faithful or responsive covenant members, excluding lost, straying, or merely vulnerable sheep elsewhere. However, the cited entry is explicitly grounded in John 10:27, and the consensus itself recognizes this same identification as central, so that possible narrowing is not sufficiently explicit to constitute a material refinement.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
